OpenSea Whitelist Checker v0.2.0
Installation and Usage Instructions
===================================

1. DOWNLOAD V0.2.0

1. Open:
   https://github.com/RohitValeecha89/opensea-whitelist-checker-releases/releases/tag/v0.2.0
2. Scroll to Assets.
3. Click OpenSea-Whitelist-Checker-Setup-0.2.0.exe.
4. Wait for the approximately 96 MB download to finish.

Do not download latest.yml or the .blockmap file. Those files are used
internally by automatic updates.


2. INSTALL THE APPLICATION

1. Double-click the downloaded EXE file.
2. If Windows SmartScreen appears:
   a. Click More info.
   b. Confirm the filename is:
      OpenSea-Whitelist-Checker-Setup-0.2.0.exe
   c. Click Run anyway.
3. Complete the installer.
4. Open OpenSea Whitelist Checker.

The SmartScreen warning may appear because the installer is not yet code-signed.


3. OBTAIN AN OPENSEA API KEY

1. Sign in to your OpenSea account.
2. Open OpenSea's developer/API section.
3. Create or request an API key.
4. Copy the key and keep it private.

Every person using the checker should use their own OpenSea API key. Do not
share the API key publicly.


4. ADD THE OPENSEA API KEY

1. Open Settings in the application.
2. Find OpenSea API key.
3. Paste your API key.
4. Leave Concurrent wallet checks at 3 initially.
5. Click Save settings.
6. Confirm that the API key shows as saved.

If OpenSea reports rate-limit errors, lower concurrency to 1 or 2.


5. ADD RPC URLS

RPCs are currently optional for eligibility checks. The OpenSea API performs
the main whitelist check, but RPCs can be saved for chain diagnostics and
future features.

Enter one RPC per line in Settings using:

chain=https://your-rpc-url

Example:

ethereum=https://your-ethereum-rpc
base=https://your-base-rpc
polygon=https://your-polygon-rpc
arbitrum=https://your-arbitrum-rpc
optimism=https://your-optimism-rpc

Important:
- Do not include spaces before the chain name.
- Every line must contain an equals sign.
- Use the chain slug expected by OpenSea.
- Keep RPC provider API keys private.

Click Save settings after adding the RPC URLs.


6. IMPORT WALLETS

SECURITY WARNING:
Only import dedicated, low-value mint wallets. Do not import treasury wallets,
hardware-wallet seed wallets, or wallets containing valuable assets.

1. Open Wallets.
2. Under Import private keys, enter a Group name such as Mint Wallets.
3. Paste one private key per line.
4. Each key must contain 0x followed by 64 hexadecimal characters.
5. Click Encrypt & import.
6. Review Imported, Duplicates, and Invalid lines.

After import, the app displays wallet addresses, not private keys. Keys are
encrypted locally using Windows encryption and cannot be displayed again from
the app.


7. ADD OR DISCOVER AN OPENSEA DROP

Automatic discovery:

1. Open Drops.
2. Click Discover.
3. Wait for OpenSea drops to load.
4. Find the collection you want.
5. Click Check ->.

Manual entry:

1. Open the collection on OpenSea.
2. Copy its collection URL, for example:
   https://opensea.io/collection/example-collection/overview
3. Return to the app and open Drops.
4. Paste the URL into Paste an OpenSea collection link or slug.
5. Click Add drop.
6. When the collection appears, click Check ->.

You may also enter only the collection slug, such as:

example-collection

Only genuine opensea.io collection links are accepted.


8. CHECK WHITELIST ELIGIBILITY

1. Open Checker.
2. Paste an OpenSea collection link, enter its slug, or select a saved drop.
3. Under Wallets, select All wallets or a specific wallet group.
4. Click Check.
5. Wait for the progress bar to finish.

The application authenticates each wallet with OpenSea using a locally signed
login message. It requests only the read:eligibility scope and does not submit
a transaction or mint anything.


9. UNDERSTAND THE RESULTS

Each row represents a wallet. Each phase has its own column.

- A green check mark with 1 means the wallet is eligible, usually with one
  remaining mint.
- A green check mark with 2 means it may mint two, depending on OpenSea's
  returned allowance.
- A dash means the wallet is not eligible for that phase or has no remaining
  allowance.
- Error means that wallet could not be checked.

Hover over Error to view the message. Common causes include:
- Invalid or expired OpenSea API key
- OpenSea rate limiting
- Drop not found
- Eligibility endpoint unavailable
- Collection not using an OpenSea-supported drop configuration


10. AUTOMATIC UPDATES

The app checks for updates shortly after opening.

When an update is available:

1. A Version X is available banner appears.
2. Click Download update.
3. Wait for the download to finish.
4. Click Restart & install.

You can also open Settings and click Check for updates. GitHub login and tokens
are not required.
